WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:04.920
Some artists say that suffering is necessary
to create meaningful art... but there have

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:05.960
to be limits, right?

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:09.820
Getting mauled by a lion, or driving a truck
off a cliff is the kind of that suffering

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:13.890
seems to be reserved exclusively for those
whose art is the Hollywood film—and for

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:18.150
better and worse, these movies were among
the most hazardous to the health of cast and

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:19.230
crew.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:20.310
Waterworld

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:26.460
While it's seen today as something of a curiosity,
1995's Waterworld was a spectacularly misguided,

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:28.909
career-derailing failure for Kevin Costner.

00:00:28.909 --> 00:00:33.750
The most expensive film ever made at the time,
it baffled audiences with its bizarre mythology

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:35.850
and uneven tone.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:36.899
"Hey!

00:00:36.899 --> 00:00:37.940
Hahah!"

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:43.920
And as might be expected from a film that
takes place on a world covered in ocean, the

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:48.780
seafaring shoot was more than a little hazardous,
and nearly killed several members of the cast

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:49.969
and crew.

00:00:49.969 --> 00:00:54.210
Everyday unpleasantness included seasickness
and constant jellyfish stings.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:58.859
One costly set sunk, and a diver suffered
an almost-lethal case of the bends during

00:00:58.859 --> 00:01:00.089
the retrieval effort.

00:01:00.089 --> 00:01:05.250
Female lead Jeanne Tripplehorn and child actress
Tina Majorino had to be rescued by divers

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:10.270
when their sailboat fell apart, and Costner
was pummeled by raging winds and seawater

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:14.500
for more than half an hour while suspended
40 feet in the air for a stunt.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:18.890
Even one experienced stuntman, famous big
wave surfer Bill Hamilton, was lost at sea

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:23.990
while commuting to the shoot on a jet ski,
and had to be rescued by a production helicopter.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:29.469
The entire shoot was one gigantic risk to
life and limb, all for a film that, well…

00:01:29.469 --> 00:01:31.740
"What do you think, Toby?

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:32.740
The truth!"

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:34.160
"… looks like s---."

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.320
The Expendables 3

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:40.110
Given the age of the cast, it stands to reason
that the Expendables films have dished out

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:45.200
a few injuries to its stars, but The Expendables
3 really seemed to have it in for its unusually

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:46.340
old cast.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:51.590
"Age is just a state of mind."

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:56.439
Then-68-year old Sylvester Stallone suffered
a scary fall that necessitated a metal plate

00:01:56.439 --> 00:02:00.340
in his back, which joined the one in his neck
from an injury suffered on the set of the

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:01.450
first film.

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:05.700
Co-star Antonio Banderas injured his knee
in his very first take.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:10.390
But the production reserved its worst mishap
for Jason Statham, who drove his truck straight

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:13.500
off a dock during a rehearsal and into the
Black Sea.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:18.360
Everybody panicked—except Statham, who calmly
swam to safety as the truck sank into the

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.360
depths.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:23.270
Still, Statham told Jimmy Fallon that he wasn't
nearly as calm headed as everyone thought.

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:28.100
"So I'm like, 60ft at the bottom of the Black
Sea, stuck in the mud, thinking, 'Hang on

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:29.100
a minute.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:30.370
How am I gonna get out of this?

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:32.160
This is how it ends!'"

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.600
While Statham called it a "nightmare," instinct
kicked in and he made it out safely.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:38.970
Co-star Terry Crews summed it up, saying,

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:41.780
"Jason Statham is a true bad, bad dude."

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:43.390
Roar

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:47.920
If ever a film seemed specifically designed
to threaten the safety of everyone involved,

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:49.720
it's 1981's Roar.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:54.230
The brainchild of actress Tippi Hedren and
her writer/producer husband Noel Marshall,

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:59.130
it's the story of a family attacked by wild
jungle animals during a stay in Africa, and

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:02.490
it was executed in the most insane fashion
imaginable.

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:06.410
Dismayed to find that you couldn't just rent
lions, Hedren and Marshall bought a ranch

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:11.890
and began raising their own, along with tigers,
leopards, cheetahs, and even African elephants.

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:15.710
Then they went ahead and shot their movie
right there on the ranch with their own children,

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:18.570
working with some of the most incredibly dangerous
animals on the planet.

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:21.740
"C'mon, you'll hurt my hand.

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:24.140
Ohh ohh ohh."

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:27.220
Of course, the injuries piled up.

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:31.230
Hedren was bitten on the head by a lion, and
Marshall was bit so many times he developed

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:32.310
gangrene.

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:36.060
Hedren's daughter Melanie Griffith received
stitches to her face and almost lost an eye

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:41.510
after an attack, and the cinematographer almost
lost his entire scalp to a lion, an injury

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:43.940
requiring over 200 stitches.

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:47.910
Many of the attacks made the final cut of
the film, which wasn't given any kind of North

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:50.630
American release until 2015.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:55.550
Years later, Hedren estimated the number of
injuries at over 100 and called Roar "the

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:58.650
most dangerous film ever made in history."

00:03:58.650 --> 00:03:59.650
Troy

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:04.870
The 2004 war epic Troy was one of the most
expensive, lavishly staged films to ever be

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:06.540
so indifferently received.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:10.570
It's mostly remembered today for the strange
fate of one of its stunt performers, and for

00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:13.870
one of the most coincidental injuries in film
history.

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:18.160
During a scene with many extras, stuntman
George Camilleri sustained a severe injury

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.750
to his lower leg, requiring surgery.

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:25.130
A couple weeks later, he was re-admitted to
the hospital, and quickly passed away from

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:30.069
a pulmonary thromboembolism, a common complication
after an injury of his type.

00:04:30.069 --> 00:04:34.469
The film's star Brad Pitt, cast as Achilles,
also somehow managed to injure his character's

00:04:34.469 --> 00:04:39.270
namesake tendon during the shoot, causing
production to be shut down for ten weeks.

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:44.020
Hurricane Marty also damaged some sets, but
Troy overcame all of these difficulties and

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:48.520
arrived triumphantly in theaters, only to
be met with a solid thump.

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:49.520
Jumper

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:54.919
The 2008 sci-fi thriller Jumper didn't cause
any problems until principal photography had

00:04:54.919 --> 00:04:57.259
wrapped, but when it did, it was lethal.

00:04:57.259 --> 00:05:01.509
Set dressers were tearing down exterior sets
in the middle of a cold Toronto winter when

00:05:01.509 --> 00:05:05.840
a freakish mishap occurred: a huge chunk of
sand and earth which was frozen to a wall

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:09.009
came unstuck, striking three set dressers.

00:05:09.009 --> 00:05:13.820
56-year old David Ritchie didn't survive,
and another man sustained serious injuries

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:15.190
to his head and shoulders.

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:19.810
Toronto police described it as a "fluke accident,"
and the incident cast a pall over the highly

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:25.330
anticipated film, which unfortunately didn't
score well with… anyone.

00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:26.330
xXx

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:30.600
It goes without saying that xXx was a dangerous
film to make, as the story of an extreme sports

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:33.060
badass who becomes a secret government agent.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:38.750
Each stunt ramps up the danger, and one spectacular
stunt unfortunately cost aerial stunt coordinator

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:44.159
Harry O'Connor his life, as director Rob Cohen
recounts on the DVD commentary.

00:05:44.159 --> 00:05:51.030
"Now, unfortunately, right there, smacking
into the bridge, breaking his neck, and dying."

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:54.330
O'Connor's stunt remained in the completed
film… right up until the point in which

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:56.030
he doesn't clear the structure.

00:05:56.030 --> 00:06:00.099
Cohen considered the stunt to be so routine
that he assigned it to his second unit, and

00:06:00.099 --> 00:06:04.229
most of the cast and crew had already completed
their work on the film.

00:06:04.229 --> 00:06:05.999
Resident Evil: The Final Chapter

00:06:05.999 --> 00:06:10.979
The Resident Evil series of films is the most
profitable video game-to-film series adaptation

00:06:10.979 --> 00:06:15.979
of all time, but 2016's Resident Evil: The
Final Chapter proved to be just as big a threat

00:06:15.979 --> 00:06:19.010
to life and limb as the film's roving zombies.

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:23.499
Stuntwoman Olivia Jackson suffered horrific
injuries when a camera rig failed to make

00:06:23.499 --> 00:06:28.610
way for her during a motorcycle stunt, requiring
her to be put into a medically induced coma.

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:33.779
Her list of injuries were extensive and gory,
including having her skin torn from her face,

00:06:33.779 --> 00:06:39.099
a severed neck artery, a shattered shoulderblade,
a collapsed lung, a brain bleed, and the loss

00:06:39.099 --> 00:06:40.349
of her left arm.

00:06:40.349 --> 00:06:44.409
Unfortunately, the incident didn't seem to
lead to increased caution on the set.

00:06:44.409 --> 00:06:49.249
Only a couple months later, crew member Ricardo
Cornelius was crushed when a Humvee slipped

00:06:49.249 --> 00:06:51.690
off of a platform and pinned him to a wall.

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:56.029
Reports of the accident leaked only after
the film had completed production.

00:06:56.029 --> 00:06:57.340
Apocalypse Now

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:02.600
Perhaps no film shoot was as legendarily taxing
on everyone involved as Francis Ford Coppola's

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:07.240
masterpiece Apocalypse Now; that everyone
survived its filming is nothing short of a

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:08.240
miracle.

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:12.199
Shooting on location in Vietnam, the production
was beset by tropical disease, and natural

00:07:12.199 --> 00:07:16.509
disasters that wiped out entire sets, but
those were the least of their problems.

00:07:16.509 --> 00:07:21.099
Coppola was running a drug-fueled, psychologically
abusive madhouse that was out of control from

00:07:21.099 --> 00:07:22.599
the very beginning.

00:07:22.599 --> 00:07:26.779
Lead actor Martin Sheen stepped straight into
the worst possible environment for him at

00:07:26.779 --> 00:07:27.779
that time.

00:07:27.779 --> 00:07:31.789
In the middle of a personal breakdown and
mired deep in alcoholism, Sheen was fed a

00:07:31.789 --> 00:07:36.789
steady diet of alcohol and abuse by Coppola,
who brought out the darkness in Sheen's character

00:07:36.789 --> 00:07:39.910
by screaming at the actor and telling him
how evil he was.

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:41.889
"Sometimes he goes too far.

00:07:41.889 --> 00:07:44.659
He's the first one to admit it."

00:07:44.659 --> 00:07:49.169
Sheen would eventually have a heart attack,
yet somehow continued on with the production.

00:07:49.169 --> 00:07:53.780
Coppola himself lost 100 pounds, and Dennis
Hopper stumbled through the shoot on an insane

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:58.860
daily regimen of a case of beer, half gallon
of rum, and three ounces of cocaine.

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:02.760
Actor Sam Bottoms spent the entire year-long
shoot tripping on LSD.

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:03.900
Hopper later commented,

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:08.129
"Ask anybody who was out there, we all felt
like we fought the war."

00:08:08.129 --> 00:08:09.129
Ben-Hur

00:08:09.129 --> 00:08:14.550
1959's Ben-Hur is well-known for its iconic
chariot race scene, which was filmed on the

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:18.710
largest set ever constructed at that time,
covering 18 acres.

00:08:18.710 --> 00:08:23.300
It was also at the time the most expensive
single scene in history at $4 million dollars,

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:25.479
and took over ten grueling weeks.

00:08:25.479 --> 00:08:30.729
The action-packed sequence was virtually unprecedented
in cinema, employing a "camera car" that put

00:08:30.729 --> 00:08:34.080
viewers right in the middle of the chaotic,
horse-drawn action.

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:38.650
Most impressive was this dangerous scene,
where Ben-Hur is nearly thrown from his chariot.

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:42.960
The stunt was performed by Joe Canutt, who
landed so hard that he really was pitched

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:45.910
violently off the chariot and in between its
two horses.

00:08:45.910 --> 00:08:51.240
"Well, it turned him a complete handspring
over the chariot, and he managed to hang with

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:55.020
one hand and get a hold of the hitch rail
and turn to flip off into the speeding wall."

00:08:55.020 --> 00:09:00.810
He sustained only a cut on his chin, but to
this day, rumors persist that studio MGM covered

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:05.150
up the death of a stunt performer—simply
because it's so hard to believe nobody died

00:09:05.150 --> 00:09:06.630
filming that scene.

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:07.860
The Conqueror

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:11.710
The second-to-last film produced by Howard
Hughes would haunt him for the rest of his

00:09:11.710 --> 00:09:12.710
life.

00:09:12.710 --> 00:09:17.280
The Conqueror was a big-budget flop featuring
a woefully miscast John Wayne as an unintentionally

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:19.040
comedic Genghis Khan.

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:22.240
During the last years of his life, Hughes
bought up all the prints he could find and

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:26.960
watched the film obsessively, perhaps mortified
by its failure, or perhaps racked with guilt

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:29.810
for having basically executed the entire cast.

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:34.280
The film was shot on location in the Utah
desert, directly downwind from the area where

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:37.743
the US government detonated over 100 atomic
bombs between 1951 and 1962.

00:09:37.743 --> 00:09:38.810
An atomic bomb at the Nevada test site, 140
miles to the west.

00:09:38.810 --> 00:09:48.160
But it's old stuff to St. George."

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:53.090
Eleven were detonated in 1953 alone, the year
before The Conqueror began production.

00:09:53.090 --> 00:09:57.370
The risks of nuclear fallout were not terribly
well-understood at the time, and the Atomic

00:09:57.370 --> 00:10:00.160
Energy Commission declared the area completely
safe.

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:02.490
They were staggeringly, horrifyingly wrong.

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:07.450
In the decades following the film's production,
no fewer than 90 cast and crew passed away

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:12.830
from cancer, including Wayne, lead actress
Susan Hayward, and director Dick Powell.

00:10:12.830 --> 00:10:18.140
A subsequent study concluded that Cold War-era
nuclear testing had killed at least 11,000

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:19.140
Americans.

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:25.440
"When you saw the tragedy in terms of the
families, it sort of… broke your heart."

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:31.970
Since 1990, Congress has paid $2 billion dollars
to residents of the fallout area in which

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:33.480
The Conqueror was filmed.

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:37.570
It was one of Hollywood's biggest turkeys,
it killed one of its biggest stars, and it

00:10:37.570 --> 00:10:41.840
just may have finished the job of driving
Howard Hughes totally insane.

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:42.940
Thanks for watching!

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:45.710
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:10:45.710 --> 00:10:47.930
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

